{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Brings.HeckeMapFaceSize","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Brings.HeckeMapFaceSize","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Fintype.BigOperators","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.universal_map_face_sizes_differ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.universal_map_face_sizes_differ","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/HeckeMapFaceSize.lean#L131-L138","doc":"**The face sizes differ, which is the whole obstruction.**\n\n`H_3`'s universal map is triangular (`p = 3`); `H_4`'s is 4-gonal (`p = 4`).\nA diagonal flip -- the move dual to one ideal tetrahedron -- is defined for the\nformer and not the latter.  Trivial as arithmetic; recorded because it is the\nload-bearing step, and because stating it makes the two `p` values explicit\nrather than leaving them in prose. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.bring_refinement_count","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.bring_refinement_count","kind":"theorem","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/HeckeMapFaceSize.lean#L120-L129","doc":"**Bring's map admits `2 ^ 30 = 1073741824` diagonal refinements.**\n\nWith `F = 30` quadrilateral faces, manufacturing a triangulation to feed the\nlayering machinery costs ~10^9 free binary choices.  Selecting among them to\nreproduce a target is *selection against the target* -- fitting under the\nNO-FITTING directive, with no continuous parameter tuned. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.diagonalRefinements_card","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.diagonalRefinements_card","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/HeckeMapFaceSize.lean#L109-L118","doc":"**A diagonal-refinement of a quadrangulation is a choice of one of the two\ndiagonals in each face**, i.e. a function from the faces to a two-element set;\nthere are `2 ^ F` of them.\n\nThis is the step that prices the transfer.  The layering theorem wants a\ntriangulated surface; a quadrangulation can be turned into one, but only by\nmaking an independent binary choice per face, and this counts those choices. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.bring_aut_orientation_preserving","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.bring_aut_orientation_preserving","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/HeckeMapFaceSize.lean#L93-L105","doc":"**The paper's 120 automorphisms is `2E`, not `4E`.**\n\nFor a regular map the orientation-preserving automorphism group acts simply\ntransitively on the `2E` *directed* edges, so `|Aut^+| = 2E`.  The flag count\nis `4E`, which counts the full group *including* reflections.\n\nAt `E = 60` these are `120` and `240`; Kattan-Singerman's `|S_5| = 120` is the\norientation-preserving count.  Both are recorded so the distinction cannot be\nre-lost -- an earlier draft of the companion probe asserted `4E = 120`, which\nis false, and the probe's own check caught it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.bring_VEF","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.bring_VEF","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/HeckeMapFaceSize.lean#L77-L91","doc":"**Bring's map has 24 vertices, 60 edges and 30 quadrilateral faces.**\n\nKattan-Singerman Theorem 2.1: the unique regular map of type `{5,4}` of genus\n4, with automorphism group `S_5`.  Their convention (stated explicitly in the\npaper) is that `{5,4}` means *faces are quadrilaterals and vertices have\nvalency 5* -- so `p = 4`, `q = 5`.\n\nGiven the two incidence relations and Euler's formula at `g = 4`, the counts\nare forced.  Note the hypotheses are exactly those of\n`regularMap_edge_relation`; nothing about hyperbolic geometry enters. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.regularMap_edge_relation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.regularMap_edge_relation","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/HeckeMapFaceSize.lean#L58-L73","doc":"**Euler identity for a regular map.**  For a map on a closed orientable\nsurface of genus `g` whose faces are all `p`-gons and whose vertices all have\nvalency `q`, the incidence counts `p * F = 2 * E` (each face has `p` edges,\neach edge borders two faces) and `q * V = 2 * E` (each vertex has `q` edge-ends,\neach edge has two ends) combine with `V - E + F = 2 - 2 * g` to give\n\n  `E * (2 * p + 2 * q - p * q) = p * q * (2 - 2 * g)`.\n\nStated over an arbitrary commutative ring: the content is the elimination of\n`V` and `F`, which needs no ordering and no division.  (§7c -- do not\nspecialise to `Real` or even `Int` here; the Bring instance below does that.) ","signatureChunk":0}]}